  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 151 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nThrough Monday: Ridging aloft will strengthen over the region \nthrough Monday while a surface high remains centered just south of \nthe CWA. Together these features will maintain warmer than normal \ntemperatures and a relatively dry airmass with a PWAT less than one \ninch. Temperatures will top out in the lower 90s Monday and the hot \ntemperatures will result in near critically low relative humidity \nprompting a \"Limited\" for Fire Danger, especially considering the \nongoing drought conditions across the CWA. /22/\n\nThrough next weekend...\n\nA prolonged dry period is in store this week into next weekend with \nhigh pressure locking in dry air over the region. High temperatures \nwill be much above average, nearing records, in the low to mid 90s \n(average is 81). Due to the dry conditions, lows will cool to low \n60s most nights, with pockets of upper 50s. Deep mixing will allow \nfor RH to dip into the 20s most afternoons throughout the week, so \nlimited fire danger should persist, especially given light rain \ntotals from Isaias. High pressure will keep winds relatively light \nand should limit greater fire concerns throughout the work week.\n\nBy next weekend, a dry cold front will increase fire concerns, with \nmuch greater wind potential. Sustained winds could approach 20mph \nwith an approximately 10mb pressure gradient associated with the \nfront. Considering antecedent dry conditions, low RH and wind, an\nupgrade to an elevated could be warranted. The conditions should \npeak Saturday. By Sunday, winds should relax substantially, but \nwith RH still in the 20s, fire danger likely persists. /SAS/",
